WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.284
Module 5: fielding,
analysis and reporting.

00:00:03.284 --> 00:00:05.727
Your survey is tested and clean.

00:00:05.727 --> 00:00:10.080
Now get it to the right people,
watch it while it's live,

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.419
and turn the answers into something the
client can act on, without exposing anyone.

00:00:16.419 --> 00:00:17.946
About forty minutes.

00:00:18.433 --> 00:00:21.261
On the Distribute tab, publish the survey.

00:00:21.261 --> 00:00:25.704
It goes live and its link is created,
but nobody is contacted yet.

00:00:25.704 --> 00:00:30.416
If you offer other languages, the
first publish translates them first,

00:00:30.416 --> 00:00:31.830
so keep the tab open.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:37.215
And if you change your mind, you can
unpublish, but only until someone finishes.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:40.369
You can share three ways, and mix them.

00:00:40.369 --> 00:00:45.366
A contact list, or roster, gives
everyone their own link that works once,

00:00:45.366 --> 00:00:50.499
attaches their demographics, and lets
SurveyCraft remind people who haven't

00:00:50.499 --> 00:00:51.115
finished.

00:00:51.115 --> 00:00:54.811
A shared link or QR code
reaches people without email,

00:00:54.811 --> 00:00:59.465
but anyone with it can answer and
SurveyCraft can't tell who's done.

00:00:59.465 --> 00:01:03.777
Email invitations send personal
links to addresses you type in.

00:01:03.777 --> 00:01:09.185
The huddle pulse used a roster for the
three hundred and twenty-two people with

00:01:09.185 --> 00:01:13.018
email, and a QR code at huddles
for the seventy without.

00:01:13.533 --> 00:01:16.662
Start from the template
SurveyCraft gives you.

00:01:16.662 --> 00:01:20.266
Its columns already match
your demographic questions.

00:01:20.266 --> 00:01:24.006
Fill in email, which is
required, and name if you like.

00:01:24.006 --> 00:01:29.310
Here's the rule that trips people up:
every other column header must match the

00:01:29.310 --> 00:01:34.343
question text exactly, and every cell
must match an answer option exactly.

00:01:34.343 --> 00:01:39.715
"Dept" won't map, and nor will "Clinics"
when the option says "Clinic Network".

00:01:39.715 --> 00:01:42.435
SurveyCraft tells you what didn't match.

00:01:42.435 --> 00:01:46.787
Fix it and upload again; people
already on the list are skipped.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:52.462
Turn on automatic reminders: every three,
five or seven days, up to three times.

00:01:52.462 --> 00:01:56.205
They go only to people on the
roster who haven't finished,

00:01:56.205 --> 00:01:59.883
and none start in the last
twelve hours before you close.

00:01:59.883 --> 00:02:04.078
People who used the shared link
can't be reminded by SurveyCraft,

00:02:04.078 --> 00:02:06.659
so remind them the way you reached them.

00:02:06.659 --> 00:02:08.918
When you're done, close the survey.

00:02:08.918 --> 00:02:12.273
Everything collected is
kept, and you can reopen it.

00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:14.981
Analytics shows a completion rate.

00:02:14.981 --> 00:02:19.410
It's people who finished, divided
by everyone who opened the survey.

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:23.188
It is not a response rate, and
Analytics doesn't show one,

00:02:23.188 --> 00:02:25.012
so you work it out yourself.

00:02:25.012 --> 00:02:30.223
For the huddle pulse, two hundred and
sixty-two people opened it and two hundred

00:02:30.223 --> 00:02:34.977
and thirty-one finished, so the
completion rate was eighty-eight percent.

00:02:34.977 --> 00:02:37.974
But three hundred and
ninety-two were invited,

00:02:37.974 --> 00:02:40.840
so the response rate
was fifty-nine percent.

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:42.468
Report the response rate.

00:02:42.967 --> 00:02:48.880
The Responses tab lists everyone who
opened the survey, with flags for review.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:51.988
Fast means someone finished very quickly.

00:02:51.988 --> 00:02:55.930
Straight-line means every
scale answer was the same.

00:02:55.930 --> 00:03:01.994
And after you run the scan for AI text,
which sends open answers to an AI model,

00:03:01.994 --> 00:03:05.026
some may be flagged as possible AI text.

00:03:05.026 --> 00:03:07.149
Flags never remove anything.

00:03:07.149 --> 00:03:08.741
Read them and decide.

00:03:08.741 --> 00:03:13.896
Someone who genuinely agrees with
everything is still a real answer.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.334
Analytics turns every
question into a chart.

00:03:17.334 --> 00:03:21.667
Cross-filters narrow every chart
to a group, like one department.

00:03:21.667 --> 00:03:26.468
If your filters leave fewer people
than the survey's minimum group size,

00:03:26.468 --> 00:03:28.735
and that's never fewer than three,

00:03:28.735 --> 00:03:33.335
the charts are replaced by a message
asking you to widen the filters.

00:03:33.335 --> 00:03:37.069
Just above the minimum, a
group can still be recognized,

00:03:37.069 --> 00:03:39.869
so think before you share a filtered view.

00:03:40.367 --> 00:03:45.353
Compare two questions builds a table,
like huddle attendance by department,

00:03:45.353 --> 00:03:48.411
and tests whether the
pattern could be chance.

00:03:48.411 --> 00:03:52.334
Statistically significant means
it's unlikely to be chance.

00:03:52.334 --> 00:03:56.057
It doesn't say the difference
is big, or what caused it.

00:03:56.057 --> 00:03:59.382
Not significant means it's
consistent with chance,

00:03:59.382 --> 00:04:02.041
which isn't proof there's no difference.

00:04:02.041 --> 00:04:06.894
Like filtered analytics, this view
protects small groups, in its own way.

00:04:06.894 --> 00:04:11.881
Small rows and columns roll into "Other",
and small cells show an asterisk.

00:04:11.881 --> 00:04:17.266
But the totals still show, so a single
hidden cell can sometimes be worked out by

00:04:17.266 --> 00:04:18.064
subtraction.

00:04:18.064 --> 00:04:20.856
Combine small categories before you share.

00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:24.599
The export gives you one row per response.

00:04:24.599 --> 00:04:30.986
It applies no minimum group size, it includes
any test responses you didn't delete,

00:04:30.986 --> 00:04:34.603
and any Editor, Admin or
Owner can download it.

00:04:34.603 --> 00:04:35.680
Viewers can't.

00:04:35.680 --> 00:04:40.220
So never send a client a raw
export of an anonymous survey.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:42.683
Send prepared tables and charts.

00:04:43.167 --> 00:04:47.812
The huddle pulse has closed, and Alex
wants three findings for the board.

00:04:47.812 --> 00:04:52.457
Read the results, write each finding
with its evidence and what it means,

00:04:52.457 --> 00:04:54.366
and add one limit you'd state.

00:04:54.366 --> 00:04:56.594
Then compare with the model answer.

00:04:57.100 --> 00:05:00.879
Taylor Brooks wants the raw
answers from four clinic managers.

00:05:00.879 --> 00:05:01.854
What do you say?

00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:04.735
Two more tools for custom work.

00:05:04.735 --> 00:05:10.542
A custom 360 uses the same guided setup
as the Leadership Mosaic: questions,

00:05:10.542 --> 00:05:15.432
how raters are chosen, participants,
raters, emails, and launch.

00:05:15.432 --> 00:05:21.697
Peers, direct reports and others are shown
as groups only once at least three have

00:05:21.697 --> 00:05:22.385
answered.

00:05:22.385 --> 00:05:27.198
For a standard leadership 360,
recommend the Leadership Mosaic.

00:05:27.198 --> 00:05:33.387
A pulse program sends a rotating few
questions from a survey on a schedule to its

00:05:33.387 --> 00:05:35.908
roster and builds a trend report.

00:05:35.908 --> 00:05:42.021
Sending only to people who finished the
first survey needs an identified survey.

00:05:42.533 --> 00:05:46.321
A good results pack has a
one-line headline, who answered,

00:05:46.321 --> 00:05:50.893
three findings with evidence and a
"so what", what you don't know yet,

00:05:50.893 --> 00:05:55.921
decisions and next steps, and how results
go back to the people who answered.

00:05:55.921 --> 00:06:00.101
Prepare the charts yourself and
check every group is big enough.

00:06:00.101 --> 00:06:01.407
Then close the loop.

00:06:01.407 --> 00:06:06.305
A short "you said, we're doing" note
is what makes people answer next time.

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:08.420
One last module check.

00:06:08.933 --> 00:06:09.746
To sum up.

00:06:09.746 --> 00:06:15.678
Publishing contacts nobody, so mix a
roster and a link to reach everyone.

00:06:15.678 --> 00:06:18.928
Roster headers must match the questions.

00:06:18.928 --> 00:06:20.635
Report response rate.

00:06:20.635 --> 00:06:24.698
Analytics hides tiny groups,
exports hide nothing,

00:06:24.698 --> 00:06:28.029
and even the comparison table needs care.

00:06:28.029 --> 00:06:30.955
Next, Module 6: your practical task.

